Z||000000||Poncho Jim||03-19-2000||05:12 PM||||I'm a Pontiac fan and own a 1990 Trans AM GTA...  I'm looking to replace my second car (a crummy Hyundai) with a Fiero.  I've always liked them since I drove a friends a couple of years ago.<P>I'm not looking for absolute perfection since this will be a project car.  I want to restore it to showroom condition like I did with the GTA.<P>Can anyone offer any tips on what to look for when I'm shopping.  I've heard a few things about rust in the trunk area and corrosion of some lines under the car.  Any other 'hidden' areas to pay attention to ?<P>Any good web sites... (I have spent a while looking).<P>I want to do as much research as I can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gokart||03-19-2000||07:33 PM||||You've found the best Fiero site in the world here Jim! Good for you and your quest for the world's most fun and best looking car! Stick around here and join a local club after getting your ride and you'll become a Fieroholic real quick. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Sign up for Fiero@onelist.com as well. You'll learn alot from this as well like am!||||reg||14
Z||000002||GT Bastard||03-19-2000||07:50 PM||||What model Fiero are you looking for? 4Cyl or 6cyl?  1988 Fiero's are generally more expensive but have better suspension, although parts are harder to come by and more expensive.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||03-19-2000||07:55 PM||||Oh yeah, and make sure you get one that hasn't exploded(like all Fiero's do...)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Poncho Jim||03-19-2000||08:16 PM||||I really don't care if it's a 4 or a 6 (I've got a 5.7L T/A to rocket around in).  With the price of gas lateley, the 4 is looking very attractive.  I just like the car for its looks, its unique two seat design and the fact that it's a Pontiac.<P>And actually I'm not too particular about the year.  I like them all..  I'm in serious shopping mode right now and hopefully, I'll be able to post a pic of the new ride soon.  Usually, the local paper is full of them, but there seems to be a bit of a shortage this spring for some reason....||||reg||9
Z||000005||GT Bastard||03-19-2000||08:20 PM||||The shortage is probably due to the fact that all Fiero's burst into flames... har har har....  If you're going to get a 4-banger, I highly recommend 85 or better.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||03-19-2000||08:46 PM||||Might I suggest that you look at buying a great looking 85 SE that has been converted to a fastback. It only has 66,000 miles on the 4 cyl and has lots of new goodies. I happen to know where you could get one at a great price. I will post pic's if you are interested.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||03-20-2000||12:06 PM||||If you are planning a full restoration anyway, rust is probably the only "hidden" problem to worry a lot about. Check underneath closely. The trunk itself and the areas on either side next to the fenders are good barometers. If these are rusting through, chances are more of the body is rusting underneath it's plastic skin.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GT||03-20-2000||01:05 PM||||Hey, I would check out Fiero Dave's Indy Fiero he is selling.  Its pretty killer and its been in good hands!  I think he wants $4500 or so.  If you didnt know, the Indy Fiero is a 84.||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||03-20-2000||01:31 PM||||As long as everybody's plugging their Fieros, Jim, you might as well check out my 88 Coupe:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/000423.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/000423.html</A> <P>It's an Ohio car, but it spent half its life in FL so it's nearly rust-free.<P>A few things to watch for in used Fieros:<P>1)  Rust usually isn't that big a problem unless a Fiero has been wrecked.  Rust is usually confined to trunk floor, stamped-steel areas under headlight buckets and surface rust on the engine cradle.<BR>2)  Watch for nose and tail pieces that don't fit quite right - chances are the car has been in a fender-bender.  Misaligned hoods, decklids and roof panels often mean the frame has been bent.  Saggy doors are common with Fieros and usually don't indicate anything worse than worn hinges.<BR>3)  Non-Fieroholics often sell V6 cars because they're ticking, or 4-bangers because they're leaking a lot of oil.  The V6 tick is usually a cracked manifold or manifold gasket, and the 4-banger oil leaks are usually from the valve-cover gasket.  You can find some great deals due to people's ignorance!<BR>4)  Fiero clutches suck.  If you buy a stickshift, chances are you'll have to put a clutch in it sometime ($300-$800, depending on whether you do the work yourself).  But the manual is a lot more fun to drive IMHO.<BR>5)  Bad headlight motors and stuck emergency brakes are common trouble spots that are fairly easy to fix.  These are great negotiating points to make a good deal even better.<BR>6)  If you buy my reliable, well-maintained, trophy-winning Coupe, my wife will let me get a GT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||1
